# FS02-060: registry-v0 is PR-based and operator-merged. There is no self-service write
# path at v0 — every change to the registry is reviewed and merged by the steward operator.
#
# Dated note, 2026-09-11: every owner below is the organisation TEAM `@purposesource/stewards`
# (id 19450495, created 2026-09-11T11:42:17Z, two members), read back read-only on this date.
# From 2026-09-08 until today these rows named the organisation OWNER's login instead, because
# the team did not exist and a CODEOWNERS file naming a team that does not exist silently
# reviews nothing — GitHub cannot request a review from an owner it cannot resolve, so a
# "required review from Code Owners" rule can be satisfied by nobody at all, which is worse
# than no file. The team the record always intended now exists (SETUP-STATUS 23.15), so the
# rows name it again. The members are not listed here: neither is a PUBLIC member of the
# organisation (`orgs/purposesource/public_members` → `[]` on this date), and this is a public
# repository, so the count is recorded and the logins stay in the private record.
#
# WHAT IS NOT TRUE YET, all of it measured read-only on 2026-09-11. Two operator acts remain,
# IN THIS ORDER, and until the first of them these rows still request review from nobody:
#
#   1. THE TEAM HOLDS NO REPOSITORY ACCESS. `orgs/purposesource/teams/stewards/repos` returns
#      `[]`, and the team object reports `repos_count: 0`. GitHub resolves a TEAM as a code
#      owner only where that team has write access to the repository — even when every member
#      already has write individually — so today these rows name an owner this repository
#      cannot resolve. Granting `@purposesource/stewards` WRITE on `registry-v0`, `spec` and
#      `license` is the first act, and it is what makes this file real rather than decorative.
#   2. NO BRANCH CARRIES A REQUIRED REVIEW. `repos/purposesource/registry-v0/branches/main/
#      protection` reports `required_pull_request_reviews: null` — the protection is linear
#      history, no force-push, no deletions, and nothing else; `spec` and `license` read the
#      same. "Require a pull request before merging" with "require review from Code Owners"
#      (1 approval) is the second act. Until it exists this file is advisory: it requests a
#      review, it does not require one.
#
# THE ORDER IS THE WHOLE POINT. Act 2 before act 1 produces a rule nobody can satisfy — the
# same "satisfied by nobody" failure the superseded note describes, in a new shape, except
# that this time it locks the admins out of their own repository instead of merely reviewing
# nothing. Grant the access, confirm on the next pull request that a review is actually
# requested of `@purposesource/stewards`, and only then require it.
#
# Both acts are the operator's, tracked as PS-O14.
#
# Superseded wording, kept as history rather than dropped: "[HUMAN TODO] Replace the
# placeholder team with the real one once the GitHub org exists and branch protection is
# configured." (until 2026-09-08), and the 2026-09-08 note that put the org owner's login
# here — its reasoning is restated above, and this change reverses its conclusion on the day
# the condition it named was met.

*                       @purposesource/stewards

# The append-only tree. Same owner today; called out separately because the review question
# is different — here the reviewer is also checking that nothing EARLIER changed, which is a
# different read from reviewing a new registry entry.
#
# Dated note, 2026-09-09: this list held `/ledger/` and `/ct/` beside it. Both trees retired
# with the move of the v0 ledger and CT log to {ORG}/website (FS-00 §6.10), and the rules are
# deleted here because a path rule for a directory that does not exist owns nothing and would
# read as though someone still watched it. Not because ownership was handed over: that
# repository has no CODEOWNERS file at this date. Its append-only guards run in its CI on
# every pull request and every push, which is the mechanical half; the reviewer's half — a
# named owner whose review GitHub requests for those paths — is nobody's there yet.
/registry/              @purposesource/stewards

